 Yeah.

00:48:29.000 --> 00:48:32.000
 The first CGI in movies was in Vertigo.

00:48:32.000 --> 00:48:34.000
 1958, Alfred Hitchcock.

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:35.000
 Oh!

00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:39.000
 And it's the swirly bit at the start, so it's not in the real film.

00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:40.000
 Oh, yeah.

00:48:40.000 --> 00:48:44.000
 And he used something called a charrison predictor,

00:48:44.000 --> 00:48:46.000
 and this was something they used in the war,

00:48:46.000 --> 00:48:50.000
 where you could put a load of inputs in about how fast an aeroplane was going,

00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:52.000
 and what direction it was going.

00:48:52.000 --> 00:48:55.000
 And it would be able to predict where the aeroplane would go,

00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:57.000
 and then you'd be able to fire in that spot,

00:48:57.000 --> 00:49:00.000
 so that when the plane got there, the bullet would get there at the same time.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:03.000
 And he basically put a load of inputs into this,

00:49:03.000 --> 00:49:06.000
 and then hung it over his camera or whatever,

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:09.000
 and made it make lots of different twists and turns.

00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:11.000
 And that's what makes those spirals at the start of that film.

00:49:11.000 --> 00:49:12.000
 That's so cool.

00:49:12.000 --> 00:49:13.000
 Oh, yeah.

00:49:13.000 --> 00:49:18.000
 One of the first films to really lean in, I suppose, to CGI,

00:49:18.000 --> 00:49:21.000
 was Tron, which I haven't seen, but it looks brilliant in 1982.

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:27.000
 So this really was the first pioneering use of computer-generated imagery

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:29.000
 without any real world in it.

00:49:29.000 --> 00:49:34.000
 But I always find with early CGI, the weird stuff is the stuff that's kind of low-fi

00:49:34.000 --> 00:49:35.000
 and done by hand.

00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:39.000
 So all the characters in it are just a face, and then the rest of them is CGI,

00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:42.000
 and they've got all these sort of glowing lines,

00:49:42.000 --> 00:49:44.000
 like lines of circuitry all over their bodies.

00:49:44.000 --> 00:49:48.000
 And every single frame of those lines was hand-painted on.

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:52.000
 So there was about half a million separate pieces of art done for that film

00:49:52.000 --> 00:49:56.000
 with little bits of paint that hand-painted on the glowing lines of the circuitry,

00:49:56.000 --> 00:49:57.000
 which was the opposite.

00:49:57.000 --> 00:50:02.000
 And then, it couldn't be nominated for an Oscar for Best Special Effects,

00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:04.000
 because it was cheating.

00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:06.000
 Yeah, I remember that.

00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:08.000
 You were 1882.

00:50:08.000 --> 00:50:09.000
 You were one.

00:50:09.000 --> 00:50:14.000
 I hadn't been born for two years, but I remember, first thing I said when I came out of the womb,

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:16.000
 going, "That's bullshit, man."

00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:21.000
 You came out of the womb, picked up your friends when this bullshit...

